

### 🔹 C++ (your code, mutable)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string myString = "Hello, World!";
    cout << myString << endl;       // Hello, World!
    cout << myString[0] << endl;    // H
    myString[3] = 'K';              // modify character at index 3
    cout << myString << endl;       // HelKo, World!
    return 0;
}
```

---

### 🔹 Java (strings are immutable)

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String myString = "Hello, World!";
        System.out.println(myString);          // Hello, World!
        System.out.println(myString.charAt(0)); // H

        // To modify a character, convert to char[] or StringBuilder
        char[] chars = myString.toCharArray();
        chars[3] = 'K';
        String modified = new String(chars);
        System.out.println(modified);          // HelKo, World!
    }
}
```

**Alternative using `StringBuilder`:**
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello, World!");
sb.setCharAt(3, 'K');
System.out.println(sb.toString());   // HelKo, World!
```

---

### 🔹 Python (strings are immutable)

```python
myString = "Hello, World!"
print(myString)          # Hello, World!
print(myString[0])       # H

# To modify, convert to list, change, then join
chars = list(myString)
chars[3] = 'K'
myString = ''.join(chars)
print(myString)          # HelKo, World!
```

**Alternative using slicing (if only one character):**
```python
myString = "Hello, World!"
myString = myString[:3] + 'K' + myString[4:]
print(myString)          # HelKo, World!
```
